 in water-rich environments. What does this suggest about Mars long ago, and why might that matter to scientists who look for life beyond Earth?"},{"id":"library:mars-red-color:5","kind":"open","max":180,"min":40,"q":"Scientists changed their idea about what colours Mars red. Why is it important to change our minds when new evidence appears? Give an example from science or from your own life."}],"source":{"license":"public domain","name":"VOA Learning English (Bryan Lynn)","note":"the broadcast sign-off removed","url":"https://learningenglish.voanews.com/a/new-discovery-could-explain-what-gives-mars-its-red-color/7990107.html"},"subtitle":"Scientists may have found the mineral that colours the Red Planet, and a clue that it once had water.","title":"Why Is Mars Red?","topic":"science","words":483}
